-- title:
--   Descent of a sum of two characters to two dimensions
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a group, $k$ a finite field, $\Omega$ a field, and $\iota : k \to \Omega$ a ring homomorphism. Let $\chi_1, \chi_2 : G \to \Omega^\times$ be group homomorphisms and assume that for every $g \in G$ both $\chi_1(g) + \chi_2(g)$ and $\chi_1(g)\chi_2(g)$, viewed in $\Omega$, lie in the image of $\iota$. Then there exist a type $V_0$ carrying the structure of an additive commutative group and of a $k$-module, together with a group homomorphism $\rho_0$ from $G$ to the group of $k$-linear automorphisms of $V_0$, such that: the $k$-dimension of $V_0$ is $2$; for every $g$ with $\chi_1(g) = 1$ and $\chi_2(g) = 1$ one has $\rho_0(g) = 1$; for every $g$, $\iota$ applied to the trace of the $k$-linear endomorphism underlying $\rho_0(g)$ equals $\chi_1(g) + \chi_2(g)$; and for every $g$, $\iota$ applied to the determinant of that endomorphism equals $\chi_1(g)\chi_2(g)$. Both $V_0$ and the fields $k$, $\Omega$ live in the lowest universe.
--
--   This is the reducible case of the descent of a two-dimensional representation to the finite field generated by its characteristic polynomials: the pair of characters $\chi_1 \oplus \chi_2$ is replaced by a genuine $k$-rational two-dimensional representation with the same trace and determinant. It is used in the construction of the mod-$p$ Galois representation attached to Hecke eigenvalue data, where it supplies the required two-dimensional $k$-representation in the case that the prescribed trace and determinant come from a pair of characters.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MonoidHom_exists_finrank_two_trace_eq_add_det_eq_mul_of_mem_range.lean

import Mathlib

